{% set local_problem %}
<p>Athens' established neighborhoods tell a story of different building eras — and different drainage standards. Homes built in the 1950s and 1960s near Athens State University often have original downspouts that were adequate for their time but can't handle today's climate expectations. Older historic homes near downtown may have systems that have been patched and modified for decades. Even newer construction sometimes cuts corners on downspout sizing.</p>

<p>The common thread: downspouts that dump water too close to the foundation. Walk through any Athens neighborhood after a heavy rain and you'll see it — water pooling at the base of homes, erosion channels in landscaping, damp spots on foundation walls. On Limestone County's clay soil, that water doesn't absorb quickly. It sits there, finding the path of least resistance, which is often your basement or crawlspace.</p>

<p>Athens' rolling topography adds another challenge. Unlike flat subdivisions where water has nowhere natural to go, Athens properties often have slopes that could help drainage — if the downspouts are positioned to take advantage of them. Unfortunately, many systems were installed without considering the property's natural grade, dumping water at high points instead of routing it downhill.</p>

<p>Properly sized and positioned downspouts solve these problems. They move water far from your foundation, work with your property's natural slope, and handle the volume that North Alabama's storms produce. It's a relatively simple improvement that prevents expensive foundation damage.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set service_explanation %}
<p>A good downspout system has three essential components: adequate capacity, proper positioning, and effective water dispersal.</p>

<p>Capacity means having enough downspouts, sized appropriately for your roof area and North Alabama's rainfall intensity. We often recommend 3x4 inch downspouts instead of standard 2x3 inch — they handle the volume during heavy storms without backing up or causing gutter overflow.</p>

<p>Positioning means placing downspouts where water naturally flows, typically at corners and low points in your gutter system, and directing them to work with your property's topography. Athens' hilly terrain can be an advantage — if we route water downhill, gravity does the work of moving it far from your home.</p>

<p>Water dispersal means getting that water far from your foundation. Basic splash blocks move water a few inches. Extensions move it a few feet. For Limestone County's clay soil, underground drains that route water 10+ feet away provide the best protection — and they're invisible once installed.</p>
{% endset %}

